- P-37 **One library, the interpreter's, serves both paths: the compiled path checks it, compiles it and runs it.** The interpreter's prelude, `FortressLibrary` and `FortressBuiltin`, becomes the compiled path's prelude in place of the compiler's own, and the compiled path reads the interpreter's library with it. The compiler's prelude, the files `CompilerLibrary`, `CompilerBuiltin` and `CompilerAlgebra`, is a bootstrap to remove: no declaration is added to it, and at the switch-over its three files are deleted and their tests kept (P-38 says which tests are respelled). Why, as the curator put it: the switch-over exists to avoid porting walk's library into the compiler's library, and the revival continues the type checker's development so that it accepts the fuller library that walk uses. The ground in the language (P-26 defines the layers): the specification names one set of default libraries, chiefly `FortressLibrary` and `FortressBuiltin`, and never names the compiler's. Beside the natives (P-33), the switch-over needs two zeros over the fourteen components, which are the distance stage's twelve and the two that `List` imports, `CovariantCollection` and `OneShotFlag`: the checker finds no error, and the code generator refuses no declaration. The decision assumes that the compiled path can run the library, not only check it. Measured: the code generator refuses 52 declarations of the twelve that the checker passes (F-34). Not measured: any component of the library compiled and run. So the premise is unmeasured, not refuted. P-33 says when the library's other components are cleared. The decision settles which files survive, not the designs inside them. The compiler's prelude belongs to the team's implementation on the JVM; the team called parts of it "bogus" and the switch to it a "hopefully temporary hack" (`WellKnownNames.java:113-116`); its designs are evidence, not authority. A prelude design that differs from the library's binds the one library in two cases only: the team's later word on the language backs it, as the exclusion rule backs the flat number types (P-43) and the team's later export check backs the declaration of abstract members (P-158); or the language's text is silent or ambiguous and the team's compiled checker enforces the design as a rule. A prelude line counts as the team's later word only if it is later than the library's text on the same shape, by P-26's dating, and no later text of the language contradicts it. A stub does not bind, and neither does a workaround of a JVM or checker gap, such as fixed arities in place of varargs. This decision does not stop work on the code generator, which is compiler work under either library. The decision is not put to the curator again. How the checker comes to accept the library, its first step and the order of the work, is the plan that the curator approved (P-33).
  by: the clarity pass; note: foundations judgement section 2 tenets 3 and 6, section 3, section 7 P-37; his reason 2026-10-26T04:47:50Z; "Only which file survives." 2026-09-24T01:02:52Z; the fourteen per the rewrite brief (open answer 1), the judgement's thirteen a miscount; no count for the other components (F-290's 40 are outside the twelve; 38 outside the fourteen); "bogus" per prelude-lens.md:66 (Ryu 2009, 525264af1); "the one library may take a design of the prelude's, as it took the prelude's flat shape of the number types" replaced by the rule of judgement section 3; origin 2026-09-20T14:33:50Z and 14:39:06Z; rewrite/c1/proposals.md; P-158 moved from the second case to the first, after the trap test (CLIMB-BATCH-22c.md, question 3)
- P-43 **The exclusion rule stays, and the number tower is flat (route A).** The compiled checker keeps the multiple instantiation exclusion rule: no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait. So the library's numbers are flat. The fixed-width types (`ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64`, `RR32`, `RR64`), `ZZ` and `QQ` are siblings under `Number`, each with its own algebra, and `Number` has no catch-all operators. The grounds are in the language (P-26 defines the layers). The type group states the rule in four texts: the 2011 paper (`Papers/Types/exclusion.tick`), the 2012 Types chapter, Karl Naden's 2012 write-up and the POPL 2019 paper. Under the rule a nested tower whose every level carries the algebra traits at its own type is impossible: if `ZZ32 extends ZZ64`, then `ZZ32` is `Integral` at two instantiations. The Working Draft's types chapter says that the numeric types "are mutually exclusive; no value has more than one of them" (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/types-vals-vars.tex`). The later texts side with that sentence against the Working Draft's library chapters, which make ℤ a subtype of ℚ (P-26). The compiler's prelude, flattened by Chase, Ryu, Maessen and Allen in 2009 and 2010, is the team's instance of the same answer on the compiled path, not a ground for it. Route C is a nested tower under the forest rule of the team's 2012 patent applications. Weigh any return to it against F-46, which gives what it would need, and P-95, which keeps it open.
